{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\noutput_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/reasoning_quality/run-20260426T075611Z/low/site/spaces/alpha/runs/run-20260426T075611Z--43fuzynxme/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\noutput_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\ndata = {\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2013\\\",\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"source_markdown\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The extracted front matter identifies Pacific Philosophical Quarterly 94 (2013) 465-485 and copyright 2013.\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Why Full Grounding Necessitates What It Grounds\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\\\"Kelly Trogdon\\\"]\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Full grounding necessitates truth\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"If a fact [p] is fully grounded in a plurality of facts D, then it is metaphysically necessary that the conjunction of the propositions corresponding to D entails p.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\\\"necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then \u000b0(∧D → p).\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-modality-cannot-define-fullness\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Fullness is explanatory\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The distinction between full and merely partial grounding is explanatory rather than purely modal, because necessary truths can have merely partial grounds while still satisfying modal entailment trivially.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\\\"Moral: the distinction between full and mere partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal one.\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Significance constrains full grounding\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Full grounding obtains only where the relevant connecting question lacks cognitive significance for fully informed rational subjects.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\\\"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance.\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-essence-explains-entailment\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Essence supplies entailment\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"When connecting questions lack cognitive significance, Fine-style essential truths about the grounded fact, its grounds, their plurality, or involved entities explain why the grounds entail the grounded proposition.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\\\"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p.\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Essence yields necessity\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Because essential truths are metaphysically necessary, the significance and essence principles together imply the modal necessity of full grounding.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\\\"(3) By (2) and essence, there are essential truths about [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. (4) The essential truths are metaphysically necessary. (5) By (3) and (4), \u000b0(∧D → p).\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Counterexamples misidentify grounds\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Putative counterexamples to the necessity of grounding typically misidentify a merely partial ground, or even a non-ground, as a full ground.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"When faced with a case in which it seems that [p] is fully grounded in D yet ~\u000b0(∧D → p), the advocate of necessity should check to see if there are additional explanatorily relevant facts P such that \u000b0(∧(D, P) → p).\\\",\n        \\\"Hence, it seems that the proponent of this putative counterexample, rather than mistaking a mere partial ground for a complete ground, mistakes a non-ground for a ground.\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"evidence_items\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-necessity-thesis-formula--18b71a159e80\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Necessity Thesis Formula\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then \u000b0(∧D → p).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This displayed formal thesis is the paper's target proposition. It fixes the exact modal sense of grounding necessity under discussion: full grounds need not guarantee that the grounding relation itself recurs, but their corresponding propositions must metaphysically entail the grounded proposition.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"p.467\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-significance-principle--a7e502b87698\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Significance Constraint\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The significance principle supplies the first substantive premise in the argument. It links metaphysical grounding to a general epistemic condition: if a connecting question remains cognitively significant even under full information about the relevant natures, the alleged full grounding relation is not present.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\", \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"p.467\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-essence-principle--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Essence Entailment Principle\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This principle connects the epistemic condition to Finean essence. It is the bridge from the absence of cognitive significance to a metaphysical basis for entailment, making the argument more than a stipulative association between grounding and modality.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"claim-essence-explains-entailment\\\", \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"pp.467, 473-474\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-five-step-necessity-argument--18b71a159e804\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Five-Step Necessity Argument\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"(1) Suppose that [p] is (fully) grounded in D. (2) By (1) and significance, the connecting question ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each member of D obtains?’ lacks cognitive significance. (3) By (2) and essence, there are essential truths about [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. (4) The essential truths are metaphysically necessary. (5) By (3) and (4), \u000b0(∧D → p).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The numbered proof is the central argumentative artifact. It shows the exact dependence of the conclusion on the two defended principles and on the modal status of essential truths, thereby clarifying why the paper treats grounding necessity as explained rather than assumed.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"proof_step\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\", \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"p.467\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-counterexample-diagnostic--a7e502b876989\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Counterexample Diagnostic\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"When faced with a case in which it seems that [p] is fully grounded in D yet ~\u000b0(∧D → p), the advocate of necessity should check to see if there are additional explanatorily relevant facts P such that \u000b0(∧(D, P) → p). If such additional facts are available, we have reason to believe that D is a mere partial ground that was misidentified as a full ground; in such a case, [p] instead is fully grounded in D, P.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This passage gives the paper's method for handling apparent failures of necessity. It does not deny the intuitions behind counterexamples outright; it asks whether the proposed ground is incomplete and whether adding explanatorily relevant facts restores entailment.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\", \\\"claim-modality-cannot-define-fullness\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"p.478\\\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\\\"type\\\": \\\"supports\\\", \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\", \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\"},\n    {\\\"type\\\": \\\"supports\\\", \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-essence-explains-entailment\\\", \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\"},\n    {\\\"type\\\": \\\"supports\\\", \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\", \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"},\n    {\\\"type\\\": \\\"qualifies\\\", \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-modality-cannot-define-fullness\\\", \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"},\n    {\\\"type\\\": \\\"defends\\\", \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\", \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"}\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Kelly Trogdon argues that full grounding is necessary in the sense that the propositions corresponding to full grounds metaphysically entail the proposition corresponding to what they ground. The argument turns on a cognitive-significance constraint on grounding and a Finean essence principle that explains why fully graspable grounding connections yield necessary entailments. Apparent counterexamples are treated as cases where a merely partial ground, or sometimes a non-ground, has been mistaken for a full ground.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\\\"code\\\": \\\"source_date_record_missing\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"The source record did not resolve a publication date, but the markdown front matter identifies the article as appearing in 2013.\\\"},\n    {\\\"code\\\": \\\"markdown_extraction_preserved_layout\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"The source was read from pdftotext markdown with preserved layout; page references are inferred from visible journal pagination in the extraction.\\\"}\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"Kelly Trogdon defends the thesis that full grounding is metaphysically necessary: full grounds entail what they ground. The explanation appeals to cognitive significance and Fine-style essence, while apparent counterexamples are diagnosed as incomplete or mistaken grounding claims.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Grounding: Necessary or Contingent? addresses a central question in contemporary metaphysics: whether grounding, a relation often invoked to explain truthmaking, composition, dependence, and metaphysical explanation, has modal consequences. Trogdon distinguishes the target thesis from stronger claims that the very same grounding relation must recur whenever the grounds obtain. The defended thesis is narrower and more important for explanation: if [p] is fully grounded in a plurality D, then it is metaphysically necessary that the conjunction of the propositions corresponding to D entails p. The paper first blocks a tempting shortcut. Full grounding cannot simply be defined as grounding plus modal entailment, because facts corresponding to necessary truths can have merely partial grounds; modal entailment alone is too coarse to mark explanatory completeness. The positive argument instead uses two substantive principles. The significance principle says that full grounding requires the relevant connecting question—why [p] obtains given the members of D—to lack cognitive significance. This is not a claim about the ignorance of a particular person, but about whether fully informed rational understanding of the relevant natures would remove substantive puzzlement. The essence principle then says that when such connecting questions lack cognitive significance, there are Fine-style essential truths about the grounded fact, its grounds, their plurality, or involved entities according to which the grounds entail the grounded proposition. Since essential truths are metaphysically necessary, full grounding yields the necessity thesis. The later discussion treats objections and counterexamples as diagnostic tests. In cases involving enabling conditions, totality facts, relational background facts, or substance-mode dependence, the proposed ground often omits explanatorily relevant material or includes something that is not doing grounding work. Thus the modal failure is not evidence against necessity; it reveals a mistaken account of the full ground. The article's practical upshot is that grounding remains more fine-grained than modality, while nevertheless imposing modal entailment when the grounds are complete.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Target Modal Thesis\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The source distinguishes several ways a relation might be necessary and selects a specific thesis for grounding. The claim is not that whenever the grounds obtain they must ground the same fact, nor merely that something is grounded. The thesis is that full grounds metaphysically entail the proposition corresponding to what they ground, making entailment a consequence of explanatory completeness rather than the whole analysis of grounding.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Why Modality Is Insufficient\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Trogdon rejects the idea that full grounding can be separated from partial grounding by modal entailment alone. If the grounded proposition is necessary, then any possible ground entails it trivially, even when the ground is only partial. This motivates a more fine-grained explanatory account: the full/partial contrast concerns what belongs in the explanation, not just what is true across possible worlds.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-modality-cannot-define-fullness\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Cognitive Significance Constraint\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper introduces connecting questions such as why a grounded fact should obtain given its alleged grounds. A genuine full grounding explanation is constrained by whether such questions would still have substantive content for fully informed rational subjects. This makes grounding sensitive to a general epistemic structure without reducing it to the psychology or limitations of any actual questioner.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Essence as the Bridge\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The essence principle supplies the metaphysical explanation of why cognitively insignificant connecting questions close off. Drawing on Fine's Aristotelian conception, Trogdon treats essential truths as real definitions of what entities are. If the relevant essential truths include that the grounds imply the grounded proposition, then the absence of cognitive significance is anchored in essence rather than in mere modal regularity.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-essence-explains-entailment\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Structure of the Proof\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The central proof combines the two principles directly. From full grounding, significance yields the lack of cognitive significance; essence then yields essential truths according to which the grounds imply the grounded proposition; because essential truths are metaphysically necessary, the desired necessity claim follows. The argument therefore aims to explain why grounding necessitates, not merely restate that it does.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-essential-truths-are-necessary\\\", \\\"claim-grounding-is-necessary\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Handling Apparent Counterexamples\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The article's later strategy is diagnostic. If an alleged full ground fails to entail the grounded proposition, one should ask whether some explanatorily relevant fact has been omitted. In many cases, adding enabling conditions, totality facts, or relational background facts restores entailment and reveals that the original candidate was only a partial ground. In the rose redness case, the alleged substance ground may not be a ground at all.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\", \\\"claim-modality-cannot-define-fullness\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\noutput_path.write_text(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + \\\"\\\\n\\\", encoding='utf-8')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
